  "output": "## Test coverage analysis — Canon\n\n**Bottom line: there is no test infrastructure and effectively no testable logic yet.** The engine (`normalize`, `blocking`, `detect`, `graph.py`'s `select_rule`/`validate`/`resolve`, all connectors, `db.py`, `main.py`, `eval/*`) is Phase 1–3 scaffolding — every function body is `raise NotImplementedError(...)`. Writing tests against stubs would just assert `pytest.raises(NotImplementedError)`, which has no value. So this isn't a \"gaps in an existing suite\" report — it's **(A) the handful of functions that do have real logic today and are untested, (B) the test harness setup itself, which is a gap**, and **(C) the tests each stub should get the moment it's implemented**, so you have them ready per AGENTS.md's phase-by-phase build order.\n\n### A) Real logic that exists today, with zero tests\n\n**1. `should_auto_apply` — `engine/agent/graph.py:40`** (the auto-apply gate; a paper-critical function)\n```python\n# engine/agent/tests/test_graph.py\nimport pytest\nfrom agent.graph import should_auto_apply, AUTO_APPLY_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD, AUTO_APPLY_MAX_SEVERITY\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"confidence,severity,expected\", [\n    (0.85, 2, True),                                    # exact threshold, boundary severity\n    (0.849999, 2, False),                               # just under confidence threshold\n    (0.95, 3, False),                                   # high confidence, severity too high\n    (1.0, 4, False),                                    # max confidence never overrides severity\n    (0.0, 1, False),                                     # confidence floor\n    (AUTO_APPLY_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD, AUTO_APPLY_MAX_SEVERITY, True),  # named-constant boundary\n])\ndef test_should_auto_apply_gate(confidence, severity, expected):\n    assert should_auto_apply(confidence=confidence, severity=severity) is expected\n```\nGap: no test pins the two thresholds to their named constants — if someone edits the magic numbers this silently changes eval results with no failing test. Also missing: negative/out-of-range confidence (should the gate defend against `confidence=1.5`? currently it won't reject it — worth an explicit decision + test).\n\n**2. `severity_for_orphan` — `engine/pipeline/detect.py:33`**\n```python\n# engine/pipeline/tests/test_detect.py\nfrom pipeline.detect import severity_for_orphan\n\ndef test_severity_for_orphan_with_children_is_4():\n    assert severity_for_orphan(has_children=True) == 4\n\ndef test_severity_for_orphan_without_children_is_3():\n    assert severity_for_orphan(has_children=False) == 3\n```\n\n**3. `rule_specificity` — `engine/pipeline/schema.py:108`** (ordering logic that decides which survivorship rule wins)\n```python\n# engine/pipeline/tests/test_schema.py\nfrom pipeline.schema import SurvivorshipRule, rule_specificity\n\ndef _rule(**kw):\n    base = dict(id=\"r1\", field=\"*\", entity_type=\"*\", strategy=\"escalate\", description=\"d\")\n    return SurvivorshipRule(**{**base, **kw})\n\ndef test_specificity_ordering_field_and_entity_both_specific_wins():\n    both = _rule(field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\")\n    field_only = _rule(field=\"territory\")\n    entity_only = _rule(entity_type=\"account\")\n    wildcard = _rule()\n    assert rule_specificity(both) > rule_specificity(field_only) > rule_specificity(wildcard)\n    assert rule_specificity(both) > rule_specificity(entity_only) > rule_specificity(wildcard)\n\ndef test_field_specificity_outweighs_entity_specificity():\n    # AGENTS.md: exact field beats exact entity_type when only one can be specific\n    field_only = _rule(field=\"territory\")\n    entity_only = _rule(entity_type=\"account\")\n    assert rule_specificity(field_only) > rule_specificity(entity_only)\n```\nGap: this test also documents/locks in a real ambiguity — AGENTS.md says \"exact field + exact entityType beats a wildcard on either,\" but doesn't explicitly rank field-only vs entity-only specificity against each other. The current weights (`field` worth 2, `entity_type` worth 1) make field win. Worth confirming that's intended before Phase 3 relies on it, since `select_rule` (still a stub) will call this for ties.\n\n**4. `SurvivorshipRule` validator — `engine/pipeline/schema.py:101-105`** (`prefer_source` requires `preferred_source_id`)\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom pydantic import ValidationError\nfrom pipeline.schema import SurvivorshipRule\n\ndef test_prefer_source_without_preferred_source_id_raises():\n    with pytest.raises(ValidationError):\n        SurvivorshipRule(id=\"r1\", field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\",\n                         strategy=\"prefer_source\", description=\"d\")\n\ndef test_prefer_source_with_preferred_source_id_ok():\n    rule = SurvivorshipRule(id=\"r1\", field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\",\n                             strategy=\"prefer_source\", preferred_source_id=\"src-a\",\n                             description=\"d\")\n    assert rule.preferred_source_id == \"src-a\"\n\ndef test_non_prefer_source_strategy_does_not_require_preferred_source_id():\n    rule = SurvivorshipRule(id=\"r1\", field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\",\n                             strategy=\"most_recent\", description=\"d\")\n    assert rule.preferred_source_id is None\n```\n\n**5. `CanonModel` camelCase wire contract — `engine/pipeline/schema.py:51-58`** (the tool-contract boundary — highest-value untested surface, since a silent break here desyncs `web/types/` from `engine/pipeline/schema.py` with no signal)\n```python\ndef test_canonical_entity_serializes_camelcase_on_wire():\n    from pipeline.schema import CanonicalEntity\n    e = CanonicalEntity(external_id=\"A1\", entity_type=\"account\", name=\"Acme\",\n                         normalized_name=\"acme\", source_id=\"s1\")\n    dumped = e.model_dump(by_alias=True)\n    assert \"externalId\" in dumped and \"external_id\" not in dumped\n\ndef test_canonical_entity_extra_field_rejected():\n    # extra=\"forbid\" — a field renamed on one side of the contract should fail loudly, not silently drop\n    import pytest\n    from pydantic import ValidationError\n    from pipeline.schema import CanonicalEntity\n    with pytest.raises(ValidationError):\n        CanonicalEntity(external_id=\"A1\", entity_type=\"account\", name=\"Acme\",\n                         normalized_name=\"acme\", source_id=\"s1\", unexpectedField=\"x\")\n\ndef test_match_confidence_out_of_range_rejected():\n    import pytest\n    from pydantic import ValidationError\n    from pipeline.schema import Match, MatchSignals\n    with pytest.raises(ValidationError):\n        Match(run_id=\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\",\n              entity_a_id=\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001\",\n              entity_b_id=\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002\",\n              confidence=1.5, method=\"exact_key\",\n              signals=MatchSignals(exact_key=True, cosine=1.0, fuzzy_ratio=100.0))\n```\n\n**6. `reviewerLabel` — `web/lib/reviewer-label.ts`** (audit-trail identity fallback chain — real logic, zero tests, and it feeds `audit_log.actor`)\n```ts\n// web/lib/reviewer-label.test.ts (needs a test runner — see gap below)\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { reviewerLabel } from \"./reviewer-label\";\n\ndescribe(\"reviewerLabel\", () => {\n  it(\"prefers username when present\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"j.rao\", primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"uid_1\"))\n      .toBe(\"j.rao\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to email when username is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"uid_1\"))\n      .toBe(\"j@x.com\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to the stable id when both are absent\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"uid_1\")).toBe(\"uid_1\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to the stable id when user is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel(null, \"uid_1\")).toBe(\"uid_1\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to id when username is empty string, not just null\", () => {\n    // edge case: \"\" is falsy in JS but not `??`'s target — confirm intended behavior\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"\", primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"uid_1\")).toBe(\"\");\n  });\n});\n```\nThe last case is worth actually deciding: `??` only falls through on `null`/`undefined`, so an empty-string username (if Clerk ever returns one) would render as a blank actor in the audit trail rather than falling back. That's a real edge case this function's own logic doesn't guard against — confirm intent, then the test either documents it or you add a guard.\n\n**7. `requireReviewer` / `getReviewer` — `web/lib/server/auth.ts`** (auth boundary — no session should never silently produce an audit-log row)\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/auth.test.ts — mock @clerk/nextjs/server\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi } from \"vitest\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\", () => ({\n  auth: vi.fn(),\n  currentUser: vi.fn(),\n}));\n\ndescribe(\"requireReviewer\", () => {\n  it(\"throws when there is no session\", async () => {\n    const { auth } = await import(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n    (auth as any).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: false, userId: null });\n    const { requireReviewer } = await import(\"./auth\");\n    await expect(requireReviewer()).rejects.toThrow(/No authenticated reviewer/);\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns userId + resolved label when authenticated\", async () => {\n    const { auth, currentUser } = await import(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n    (auth as any).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: true, userId: \"uid_1\" });\n    (currentUser as any).mockResolvedValue({ username: \"j.rao\", primaryEmailAddress: null });\n    const { requireReviewer } = await import(\"./auth\");\n    await expect(requireReviewer()).resolves.toEqual({ userId: \"uid_1\", label: \"j.rao\" });\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"getReviewer\", () => {\n  it(\"returns null instead of throwing when signed out\", async () => {\n    const { auth } = await import(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n    (auth as any).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: false, userId: null });\n    const { getReviewer } = await import(\"./auth\");\n    await expect(getReviewer()).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**8. `listSources` / `countSourcesBySide` — `web/lib/server/sources.ts`** (needs a DB — this is the one integration-test gap that already exists, not a future one)\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.integration.test.ts — against a real test Postgres (Drizzle), not a mock\ndescribe(\"countSourcesBySide\", () => {\n  it(\"classifies salesforce/hubspot as crm, databricks/snowflake as warehouse, synthetic separately\", async () => {\n    // seed sources of each kind, then:\n    const counts = await countSourcesBySide();\n    expect(counts).toEqual({ crm: 2, warehouse: 2, synthetic: 1 });\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns zeros when no sources are configured\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n\n  it(\"parses every row through sourceSchema — a row that fails Zod validation should throw, not silently drop\", async () => {\n    // insert a row with a malformed `config` shape directly via the db client,\n    // bypassing sourceSchema, and assert listSources() rejects rather than returning it\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### B) Test-harness gaps (block everything above)\n\n| Layer | Gap | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `web/` | No test runner in `package.json` — no vitest/jest devDependency, no config, no `test` script | You'll need to add one before any of the skeletons above can run. Recommend **Vitest** (fastest fit for a Next.js App Router + TS project, no Babel config needed) — **naming this per AGENTS.md's \"ask before adding a dependency\" rule, not installing it.** |\n| `engine/` | `pytest` is already a dev dependency but there's no `tests/` directory, no `conftest.py`, no `pytest.ini`/`[tool.pytest.ini_options]` in `pyproject.toml`, and no `pythonpath` config for the `agent`/`pipeline`/`connectors` imports (which are absolute, e.g. `from pipeline.schema import ...`) | Add `[tool.pytest.ini_options] pythonpath = [\".\"]` and a `tests/` tree mirroring the module tree, matching AGENTS.md's own `packages = [\"agent\", \"connectors\", \"eval\", \"pipeline\"]` list. |\n| both | No CI wiring runs either suite | Not yet applicable until a runner exists, but flagging so it isn't forgotten once one does. |\n\n### C) Tests to write the moment each stub is implemented (Phase 2/3 checklist)\n\nThese aren't written yet because there's nothing to call — but AGENTS.md's own build order tells you exactly what each will need to assert, so listing them now means Phase 2/3 isn't \"implement then figure out tests\":\n\n- **`normalize.fold_name`**: case folding, whitespace collapse, punctuation strip, each legal suffix in `LEGAL_SUFFIXES` (including the two-word `\"pvt ltd\"` ordering relative to bare `\"pvt\"` and `\"ltd\"`), a name with *no* foldable suffix (identity), a name that's suffix-only (e.g. `\"Inc.\"` alone — should not fold to empty), Unicode/diacritics if any org names have them.\n- **`normalize.normalize`**: empty input iterable → `[]`; a record missing an expected key (error handling — does it raise a clear error or a bare `KeyError`?); folding recorded not destructive (`name` ≠ `normalizedName` survives to the output entity).\n- **`blocking.block`**: exact-key match takes priority over embedding candidates (per AGENTS.md ordering); `top_k` boundary (fewer candidates available than `top_k`); two disjoint sides (`side_a`/`side_b` share nothing) → no false matches; **O(n·k) not O(n²) is a stated invariant — a perf/complexity regression test belongs here**, e.g. asserting call count to the scoring function scales with `n·k` not `n²` for a synthetic size sweep.\n- **`blocking.combine`**: weight sweep boundaries (0.0 and 1.0 signals), since `eval/` sweeps these weights and a silently-changed formula invalidates reported numbers without any test noticing.\n- **`detect.classify`**: one case per row of the severity table (structural/4, ownership-attribute/3, other-attribute/2, cosmetic/1) — and specifically the case where two values are literally different strings but fold to the same `normalized_name` (must classify cosmetic, not attribute).\n- **`detect.detect`**: orphan and duplicate paths (both currently unimplemented and untested even in shape) — duplicate is \"always severity 4,\" which is a strong, testable invariant.\n- **`graph.select_rule`**: no rule matches → `None` (escalates); two rules tie in specificity → first-authored wins (author-order determinism); wildcard-only ruleset.\n- **`graph.validate`**: this is the single most important test in the whole resolution agent per AGENTS.md rule #2 — **a proposed value that is neither observed value, nor a legal fold of either, must be rejected.** Write this test before the implementation exists, as an acceptance gate: `validate(\"ACC-9999\", value_a=\"ACC-1\", value_b=\"ACC-2\")` must be `False` even if a future implementation is tempted to be lenient.\n- **Connectors (`salesforce.py`, `warehouse.py`, `synthetic.py`)**: each `extract()` should be tested against the `Connector` protocol itself (`isinstance(conn, Connector)` via `runtime_checkable`), plus an empty-source case and a malformed-record case (does a connector normalize errors, or let a raw API exception leak across the layer boundary?).\n- **`main.execute_run`**: the AGENTS.md-stated invariant \"a run that fails halfway leaves a readable trail\" is directly testable once implemented — simulate a mid-pipeline exception and assert `runs.status` lands on `\"failed\"` with `error` populated, not left on the last successful stage.\n- **`db.py` writers**: table-ownership boundary tests — assert `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` never touch `sources`/`rulesets` (e.g. via a query-log spy against the test DB), since that boundary is enforced by convention only, not by a DB permission.\n\n**Recommendation**: since the codebase is pre-Phase-2, the highest-leverage next step isn't filling test gaps in nonexistent logic — it's standing up the two harnesses (B) and landing the six tests in (A) now, so Phase 2 implementations land with `pytest`/`vitest` already green and each new function gets a test in the same commit per AGENTS.md's development philosophy.\n",
